WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.916
Module 3: fielding the study.

00:00:01.916 --> 00:00:05.154
Every promise on the first
screen has to be true.

00:00:05.154 --> 00:00:10.044
In about twenty-five minutes you'll
learn to choose the band and audience,

00:00:10.044 --> 00:00:14.736
plan around the deadline, write a
welcome text that keeps its promises,

00:00:14.736 --> 00:00:16.718
and protect your storytellers.

00:00:17.233 --> 00:00:20.078
In Mosaic Signal, the unit is the story.

00:00:20.078 --> 00:00:23.990
Each story is one response,
and on an anonymous survey,

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:27.831
SurveyCraft doesn't stop
someone sharing a second one,

00:00:27.831 --> 00:00:31.316
even with ballot-stuffing
prevention switched on.

00:00:31.316 --> 00:00:35.441
That's usually fine: a second
story is still a real story.

00:00:35.441 --> 00:00:38.642
But it means you can't
quote a response rate,

00:00:38.642 --> 00:00:41.913
and you should never
write "percent of staff".

00:00:41.913 --> 00:00:46.323
Say a hundred and twenty-four of
two hundred and four stories,

00:00:46.323 --> 00:00:48.528
not sixty-one percent of staff.

00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:52.106
The band you buy is a
cap on completed stories.

00:00:52.106 --> 00:00:56.029
When the survey reaches it, new
respondents are turned away,

00:00:56.029 --> 00:01:00.409
and owners get an email at eighty
percent and again at one hundred.

00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:03.220
Here's the catch: you
can't raise it later.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:06.358
There's no self-serve
upgrade for Mosaic Signal,

00:01:06.358 --> 00:01:11.327
so choose the band from a realistic
estimate of stories, with some headroom.

00:01:11.327 --> 00:01:15.380
Ask the people who hold the
stories, not necessarily everyone.

00:01:15.380 --> 00:01:20.415
And make sure every group you plan to
discuss can reach at least ten stories.

00:01:20.415 --> 00:01:24.926
A cap that fills in the first week
hears only the fastest responders.

00:01:25.433 --> 00:01:29.432
You know the rule: launch within
about two months of purchase.

00:01:29.432 --> 00:01:34.721
In practice, owners get a reminder two
weeks before the deadline and another three

00:01:34.721 --> 00:01:37.818
days before, and a banner
appears on the survey.

00:01:37.818 --> 00:01:42.139
If it's still a draft when the
deadline passes, it becomes Expired,

00:01:42.139 --> 00:01:43.816
and Mosaic staff are told.

00:01:43.816 --> 00:01:46.074
Each extra wave gets its own clock.

00:01:46.074 --> 00:01:51.363
Northwind bought the decisions launch on
the first of December and launched on the

00:01:51.363 --> 00:01:53.943
eleventh of January, with time to spare.

00:01:53.943 --> 00:01:57.877
Put the launch date in the
client's calendar the day you buy.

00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:01.820
How you send the link
decides what you can promise.

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:07.440
An anonymous link, a QR poster, or an email
carrying that link collect no identity.

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:13.060
A tracked roster invitation records who
took part, but with anonymous responses on,

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.362
it isn't linked to what they said.

00:02:15.362 --> 00:02:20.170
Switch anonymous responses off, and
you can't call it anonymous at all.

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:22.607
The channel also shapes who answers.

00:02:22.607 --> 00:02:26.332
Links shared at huddles reach
people who go to huddles.

00:02:26.332 --> 00:02:31.884
Northwind's second change wave was collected
mostly at the new cross-site huddles,

00:02:31.884 --> 00:02:35.066
which is exactly where
official news is shared.

00:02:35.066 --> 00:02:38.994
That alone could move a slider
about how people hear news.

00:02:38.994 --> 00:02:42.853
Use more than one channel,
and write down which you used.

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:46.766
Culture Dig's story page
adds disclosures for you.

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:51.050
A standalone survey doesn't, so
you write a short welcome text.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.741
Cover six things: how long it takes,
what to share, what's collected,

00:02:55.741 --> 00:03:00.161
what not to write, who reads the
stories and how they'll be used,

00:03:00.161 --> 00:03:01.792
and that it's voluntary.

00:03:01.792 --> 00:03:07.232
The most important line is "please write
about what happened rather than who was

00:03:07.232 --> 00:03:12.671
involved", because a detailed story can
identify its author however it's stored.

00:03:12.671 --> 00:03:14.915
One more thing: after submitting,

00:03:14.915 --> 00:03:18.450
respondents get a link to
delete their own response,

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:22.054
so your story count can dip
slightly after you close.

00:03:22.567 --> 00:03:26.573
Before launch, preview it on
a phone and take it yourself.

00:03:26.573 --> 00:03:30.649
Generate some test responses
to check every question saves.

00:03:30.649 --> 00:03:36.382
They're marked as tests and never count,
not in the charts and not against the cap.

00:03:36.382 --> 00:03:40.458
Delete them anyway, so your
export holds only real stories.

00:03:40.458 --> 00:03:46.122
Check that anonymous responses match your
promise, and that Shuffle Blocks is off.

00:03:46.122 --> 00:03:49.092
Once the link is out,
freeze the questions.

00:03:49.092 --> 00:03:54.688
Change a stone or a triad label mid-way
and early and late stories were answering

00:03:54.688 --> 00:03:58.832
different questions, with nothing
on the charts to warn you.

00:03:58.832 --> 00:04:03.392
During fielding, watch the trend,
send one reminder about halfway,

00:04:03.392 --> 00:04:08.020
and read the first ten stories to
catch any misunderstanding early.

00:04:08.533 --> 00:04:10.142
Three real fielding calls.

00:04:10.142 --> 00:04:12.060
For each, choose what you'd do.

00:04:12.567 --> 00:04:16.554
One rule above all: never try
to identify a storyteller,

00:04:16.554 --> 00:04:18.904
and never help anyone else do it.

00:04:18.904 --> 00:04:21.609
Stories carry more detail than scores.

00:04:21.609 --> 00:04:25.881
The pharmacist on the night
shift at one site is one person,

00:04:25.881 --> 00:04:27.946
however anonymous the survey.

00:04:27.946 --> 00:04:32.361
When you quote, paraphrase and
strip out names, places, dates,

00:04:32.361 --> 00:04:37.558
roles and rare events, and only quote
from groups of ten stories or more.

00:04:37.558 --> 00:04:42.115
And when someone asks "who said
this?", the answer is always no,

00:04:42.115 --> 00:04:47.883
with the reason: people told their stories
because they were promised it wouldn't

00:04:47.883 --> 00:04:49.164
come back to them.

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:53.335
Here's your Fielding Checklist,
from planning to closing.

00:04:53.335 --> 00:04:54.107
Download it.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.440
Now the module check: five
questions, four to pass.

00:04:58.933 --> 00:04:59.573
To recap.

00:04:59.573 --> 00:05:01.421
Count stories, not people.

00:05:01.421 --> 00:05:06.396
The band caps completed stories and
can't be raised, so estimate well.

00:05:06.396 --> 00:05:09.452
Book the launch inside
the two-month clock.

00:05:09.452 --> 00:05:13.787
Make the welcome text's promises
match how you send the link.

00:05:13.787 --> 00:05:18.336
Delete test responses and freeze
the questions once you're live.

00:05:18.336 --> 00:05:20.681
And never identify a storyteller.

00:05:20.681 --> 00:05:22.600
Next, reading the patterns.
